    <p>
      <alert>Step 1: Perform indicated operations</alert><br />
      To add, subtract, multiply, or divide functions, we simply apply the operations to their algebraic rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><m>f+g = $ans1</m></li>
      <li><m>f-g = $ans3</m></li>
      <li><m>f \cdot g = $ans5</m></li>
      <li><m>\frac{f}{g} = $ans7</m></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <alert>Step 2: Find the domains</alert><br />
      First, let's find the domains of the individual functions <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For <m>f(x) = \sqrt{$a - x}</m>, the expression under the square root must be non-negative:
      <me>$a - x \ge 0 \implies x \le $a</me>
      So, the domain of <m>f</m> is <m>(-\infty, $a]</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For <m>g(x) = \sqrt{$b - x^2}</m>, the expression under the square root must also be non-negative:
      <me>$b - x^2 \ge 0 \implies x^2 \le $b \implies -$sqrtB \le x \le $sqrtB</me>
      So, the domain of <m>g</m> is <m>[-$sqrtB, $sqrtB]</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the sum, difference, and product (<m>f+g</m>, <m>f-g</m>, and <m>f \cdot g</m>), the domain is the intersection of the domains of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>:
      <me>(-\infty, $a] \cap [-$sqrtB, $sqrtB] = [-$sqrtB, $a]</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      For the quotient <m>\frac{f}{g}</m>, we must additionally exclude any values where the denominator <m>g(x) = 0</m>. 
      Since <m>g(x) = 0</m> at <m>x = -$sqrtB</m> and <m>x = $sqrtB</m>, we change the closed bracket at <m>-$sqrtB</m> to an open parenthesis. 
      (Note that <m>x = $sqrtB</m> is already excluded by the restriction <m>x \le $a</m>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, the domain of <m>\frac{f}{g}</m> is <m>(-$sqrtB, $a]</m>.
    </p>
  </solution> -->
  
</problem>